Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Presents case report of 22-year-old unmarried male and graduate law student, with a history of impulsivity and anxiety traits. The current presentation was with a five-month history of pervasive low mood, anhedonia, anergia, guilt, suicidal ideation, and decreased sleep and appetite. Persistent depressive symptoms despite two weeks of treatment led to the consideration of rTMS as an augmentation strategy. The stimulation parameters employed bilateral sequential theta burst protocol (1800 pulses each of intermittent TBS at left DLPFC followed by continuous TBS at right DLPFC). . During the 13th train of the fourth session (cTBS session to right DLPFC), the patient experienced a pulling sensation at the left jaw, followed by clonic facial grimace and subsequent tonic-clonic movements of the left upper limb lasting 60 seconds. He was observed to be confused for about a minute thereafter. TMS was immediately discontinued, and supportive measures were initiated. The interplay between rTMS-induced serotonin pathway modulation and lowered seizure threshold warrants further investigation. Considering the neuromodulatory effects of TMS on neurotransmitter systems, particularly serotonin, caution may be advised, especially in cases with multiple serotonergic antidepressants use and suprathreshold cTBS. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
