42. 
TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2021>
ID  - 2021-50284-027
T1  - The effects of accelerated intermittent theta-burst transcranial magnetic stimulation of the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex on executive functions in treatment-resistant depression.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Cherian, Kirsten Nicole
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Recent studies of theta burst transcranial magnetic stimulation (TBS) of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (dlPFC) have demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of depression. It is thought that high frequency transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) does not negatively affect cognition and has the potential to improve it. This study employed a novel accelerated intermittent TBS protocol to treat refractory depression, administering 90,000 pulses to the brains of depressed patients within five consecutive days. To date there has been no study with such an accelerated aiTBS protocol. This study evaluated patients with treatment-resistant depression (N=22) who underwent mood and cognitive testing prior to and following a course of aiTBS. The analyses were focused on executive functions. It was hypothesized that patients would show improvement in cognitive inhibition, cognitive switching, and working memory, and would have fewer errors during post-treatment testing. It was further hypothesized that the cognitive skill improvements would be related to improvements in mood. Results revealed significant improvements in mood following treatment and significant improvements in cognitive inhibition, cognitive switching, and problem solving. Patients also made fewer errors during post-treatment testing. Correlations and mixed effects analyses did not reveal clear relationships between the change in cognitive measures and the change in mood measures. Given the common brain circuits and structures involved in both TMS and executive functions, there may be parallel circuits that change independent of each other, there may not be a relationship between mood and cognitive improvements, or there may be a relationship that is not clear at this time. Further studies are recommended to replicate the findings and add to the power of the study. Clinical implications of aiTBS suggest it is a modality to treat both mood and cognitive skills concurrently, which may assist in reducing depression relapse rates. Some patients presented with baseline cognitive dysfunction that was normalized following treatment. aiTBS may be a novel treatment for those with neurocognitive disorders. This would merit further investigation. To elucidate the mechanisms of change, the results of the current study should be compared to findings from neuroimaging studies. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
