T1  - PFC neuromodulation with theta burst stimulation to impact behavior and neural network activity in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder.
A1  - Ferrarelli, Fabio
A1  - Phillips, Mary L
A2  - Blumberger, D. M., Vila-Rodriguez, F., Thorpe, K. E., Feffer, K., Noda, Y., Giacobbe, P., et al., Blumberger, Cheng, C. M., Li, C. T., Tsai, S. J., Cheng, Ferrarelli, F., Phillips, M. L., Ferrarelli, Huang, Y. Z., Edwards, M. J., Rounis, E., Bhatia, K. P., Rothwell, J. C., Huang, McGirr, A., Vila-Rodriguez, F., Cole, J., Torres, I. J., Arumugham, S. S., Keramatian, K., et al., McGirr, Suppa, A., Huang, Y. Z., Funke, K., Ridding, M. C., Cheeran, B., Di Lazzaro, V., et al., Suppa
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - The article briefs about the effectiveness of the PFC neuromodulation with theta burst stimulation in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. Theta Burst Stimulation (TBS) is a repetitive Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (rTMS) neuromodulation protocol that employs short bursts (three, 50 Hz pulses) delivered at 5 Hz. Intermittent TBS (iTBS) increases, and continuous TBS (cTBS) decreases, cortical neuron excitability. While TBS has been traditionally applied to the motor cortex, more recently both iTBS and cTBS paradigms have been employed over the prefrontal cortex (PFC) in healthy and psychiatric populations, including patients with Schizophrenia (SCZ) and Bipolar Disorder (BD). Specifically, in individuals with SCZ, TBS over PFC has been recently used to target negative symptoms (anhedonia, avolition), and cognitive deficits (attention, working memory). Regarding mood disorders, TBS of PFC has been primarily employed in Major Depressive Disorder individuals, and the safety and efficacy of iTBS was lately established in individuals with BD depression. Yet, despite some promising initial results, there is a need to replicate these findings in larger groups of patients, ideally with the following methodological enhancements to establish treatment effectiveness in SCZ and BD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
