T1  - Impairment of motor cortex plasticity in Parkinson's disease, as revealed by theta-burst-transcranial magnetic stimulation and transcranial random noise stimulation.
A1  - Stephani, Caspar
A1  - Nitsche, Michael A
A1  - Sommer, Martin
A1  - Paulus, Walter
A2  - Gamboa, O.L., Antal, A., Moliadze, V., Paulus, W., Gamboa, Huang, Y.-Z., Edwards, M.J., Rounis, E., Bhatia, K.P., Rothwell, J.C., Huang, Kuo, M.-F., Paulus, W., Nitsche, M.A., Kuo, Lefaucheur, J.-P., Lefaucheur, Terney, D., Chaieb, L., Moliadze, V., Antal, A., Paulus, W., Terney
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - This study evaluated stimulation-induced cortical plasticity of Theta-burst stimulation (iTBS) and transcranial random noise stimulation (tRNS) in patients with Parkinson's disease (PD). Eight patients with non-tremor-dominant idiopathic PD participated. Exclusion criteria, among others, were therapy with amantadine, anticonvulsants; dementia, dyskinesia; electrical implants or metallic material inside the head. The study was approved by the ethics committee of the University of Gottingen, and conforms to the Declaration of Helsinki. All patients gave their informed consent prior to participation in the study. Result demonstrates that a plasticity-inducing effect of iTBS is de facto absent in this group of patients with PD and tRNS reduced cortical excitability as compared to pre-stimulation baseline contrasting results on healthy subjects. L-dopa has the dose-dependent potential to reverse transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS)-generated facilitatory plasticity into inhibition in healthy subjects. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
