A2  - Brunoni, A.R., Valiengo, L., Baccaro, A., et al., Brunoni, De Ridder, D., Vanneste, S., Weisz, N., et al., De Ridder, Faber, M., Vanneste, S., Fregni, F., De Ridder, D., Faber, Frank, E., Schecklmann, M., Landgrebe, M., et al., Frank, Fregni, F., Marcondes, R., Boggio, P.S., et al., Fregni, Kim, H.J., Lee, H.J., An, S.Y., et al., Kim, Mori, F., Codeca, C., Kusayanagi, H., et al., Mori, Nitsche, M.A., Boggio, P.S., Fregni, F., Pascual-Leone, A., Nitsche, Shekhawat, G.S., Stinear, C.M., Searchfield, G.D., Shekhawat, Vanneste, S., Plazier, M., Ost, J., van der Loo, E., Van de Heyning, P., De Ridder, D., Vanneste
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Present study discusses about daily transcranial direct current stimulation (tdcs) sessions targeting the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Tinnitus is a common condition that affects approximately 20.7% of the general population, according to a recent study. In conclusion, five consecutive bifrontal tDCS sessions, with the right anode-left cathode montage previously reported as having an acute beneficial effect after just one tDCS session did not result in significant improvement. This is, to our knowledge, the first double-blind, placebo-controlled study to employ daily tDCS sessions targeting prefrontal areas for treatment of chronic tinnitus, although a daily stimulation regimen has already become common practice in studies of other neuropsychiatric disorders, such as chronic pain and depression. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
