T1  - Omega-3 fatty acids for treating residual depressive symptoms in adult patients with bipolar disorder: A systematic review and meta-analysis of double-blind randomized, placebo-controlled trials.
A1  - Kishi, Taro
A1  - Sakuma, Kenji
A1  - Okuya, Makoto
A1  - Ikeda, Masashi
A1  - Iwata, Nakao
AI  - Kishi, Taro; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9237-2236
AI  - Ikeda, Masashi; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6237-2449
A2  - GrossoG, PajakA, MarventanoS, et al., Grosso, HigginsJ, ThomasJ, ChandlerJ, et al., Higgins, RosenblatJD, KakarR, BerkM, et al., Rosenblat
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - This letter presents a systematic review and meta-analysis of double-blind randomized, placebo-controlled trials (DBRPCTs) regarding the safety and efficacy of Omega-3 fatty acids for treating residual depressive symptoms in adult patients with bipolar disorder (BD). The authors performed a systematic review and random-effects model meta-analysis of the DBRPCTs for adjunctive treatment of omega-3 for BD under the treatment as usual (pharmacotherapy using mood stabilizers, antipsychotics or antidepressants, or both or non-pharmacotherapy). The authors detected eight DBRPCTs of DHA, EPA, or both DHA and EPA treatment that met the inclusion criteria. The severity of depression in the DBRPCTs participants included in the meta-analysis was mild. The meta-analysis detected that omega-3 was superior to placebo in the improvement of RDSs and all-cause discontinuation. Because two studies contributed to the selection or attrition biases, the authors conducted a sensitivity analysis excluding these studies from the primary outcome. This sensitivity analysis revealed the superiority of omega-3s over placebo, but considerable heterogeneity disappeared, suggesting that these studies' data might cause considerable heterogeneity. Thus, the tudy exhibited that omega-3s adjunctive therapy positively contributes to RDSs treatment in BD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
